WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.415
Lesson 0.0.

00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:06.075
The Mastery Starter for Module 0, Crypto From Zero.

00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:08.730
If you're brand new, this is where you start.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.180
In the next 15 minutes, we'll turn the whole

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.120
module into pictures and numbers you can actually see.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.710
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.803
First, the 60-second version of the whole

00:00:19.803 --> 00:00:22.755
module, told as the journey of $100.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:24.432
Second, the five words you'll

00:00:24.432 --> 00:00:26.130
need, each one drawn out.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.545
Third, what to have ready before you start:

00:00:28.545 --> 00:00:30.930
your budget, your time and your tools.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.376
And fourth, your first safe step, the mastery

00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:36.140
ladder, and exactly how you'll know you've finished.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.005
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:42.413
Crypto is money recorded on a public

00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:45.055
ledger that nobody can quietly edit.

00:00:45.055 --> 00:00:47.504
And in this module, you'll learn to use it

00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:50.010
safely, with a small amount and a checklist.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.549
To make that real, let's follow $100.

00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:57.709
It starts in your bank account, as ordinary money.

00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:59.531
You send it to an exchange, a

00:00:59.531 --> 00:01:02.564
company that swaps ordinary money for crypto.

00:01:02.564 --> 00:01:04.389
You buy a little crypto there.

00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:05.646
Then you move it into a

00:01:05.646 --> 00:01:07.464
wallet that only you control.

00:01:07.464 --> 00:01:09.953
And finally, you make one small practice

00:01:09.953 --> 00:01:12.320
transaction in DeFi, the world of apps

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.959
that run on the blockchain.

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:17.744
But here's something most beginners never see.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:20.779
Your $100 shrinks a little at every step.

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:22.884
Let's put example numbers on it.

00:01:22.884 --> 00:01:25.416
The spread, the gap between the buying and

00:01:25.416 --> 00:01:28.189
selling price, might cost around 50 cents.

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:31.389
An instant-buy fee might be about $1.50.

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:33.515
And the withdrawal to your wallet, the

00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:35.559
network fee, might be around $1.

00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:37.524
That's roughly $3 in total.

00:01:38.824 --> 00:01:40.877
So you put in $100, and

00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:43.999
about $97 arrives in your wallet.

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:46.801
That isn't a scam, it's how the plumbing works.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.365
But it's why Lesson 0.3 teaches you

00:01:49.365 --> 00:01:52.499
to compare offers by what actually arrives,

00:01:52.499 --> 00:01:54.636
not by the price on the chart.

00:01:55.937 --> 00:01:57.062
And the route you choose

00:01:57.062 --> 00:01:58.757
matters more than people think.

00:01:58.757 --> 00:02:01.213
Buying with the big, friendly instant-buy

00:02:01.213 --> 00:02:03.151
button often costs the most.

00:02:03.151 --> 00:02:06.771
Using the exchange's advanced trading screen, with a simple

00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:09.726
limit order, often costs a fraction of that.

00:02:09.726 --> 00:02:11.066
Same exchange.

00:02:11.066 --> 00:02:12.206
Same coin.

00:02:12.206 --> 00:02:13.491
A different button.

00:02:13.491 --> 00:02:15.628
In this example, that's about

00:02:15.628 --> 00:02:17.480
$3 against about 60 cents.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.555
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:02:21.855 --> 00:02:23.600
Word one: blockchain.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.775
A shared ledger kept by thousands of computers.

00:02:26.775 --> 00:02:28.670
Here's what happens to a payment.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.385
You send it, and it's broadcast to the network.

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:33.579
Thousands of computers check it against

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:35.250
their copy of the ledger.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.835
It's sealed into a new block with other payments.

00:02:37.835 --> 00:02:40.510
And every copy of the ledger adds that block.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.537
Nobody is in charge, and nobody

00:02:42.537 --> 00:02:44.627
can quietly change it afterwards.

00:02:45.927 --> 00:02:47.792
And here's why it's called a chain.

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:50.637
Each block carries a seal made from its contents,

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:53.042
plus the seal of the block before it.

00:02:53.042 --> 00:02:55.215
Change one old entry and its seal

00:02:55.215 --> 00:02:57.708
changes, so every block after it stops

00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:00.692
matching, and every other copy rejects it.

00:03:00.692 --> 00:03:02.168
That's what we mean by a

00:03:02.168 --> 00:03:03.999
ledger nobody can quietly edit.

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:06.969
Word two: wallet.

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:09.009
A wallet is an app or device that

00:03:09.009 --> 00:03:11.709
holds the keys that control your crypto.

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:14.254
The coins themselves live on the blockchain.

00:03:14.254 --> 00:03:16.072
The wallet holds a key, and the

00:03:16.072 --> 00:03:18.134
key proves the coins are yours.

00:03:18.134 --> 00:03:20.639
Your seed phrase creates your private key.

00:03:20.639 --> 00:03:23.172
Your private key creates your public address,

00:03:23.172 --> 00:03:25.399
the one you share to receive money.

00:03:25.399 --> 00:03:27.414
It only works in one direction.

00:03:27.414 --> 00:03:28.903
Nobody can work backwards from

00:03:28.903 --> 00:03:30.104
your address to your key.

00:03:31.404 --> 00:03:34.119
If you remember one picture, make it this one.

00:03:34.119 --> 00:03:36.227
Your address is a mailbox slot:

00:03:36.227 --> 00:03:38.174
anyone can drop coins in.

00:03:38.174 --> 00:03:40.015
Your private key is the only

00:03:40.015 --> 00:03:41.776
key that opens the box.

00:03:41.776 --> 00:03:44.696
And your seed phrase is the master recipe that

00:03:44.696 --> 00:03:47.616
can rebuild every key, on any device, anywhere.

00:03:48.915 --> 00:03:50.910
Word three: seed phrase.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.790
12 to 24 words that are your wallet.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.570
The words come from a standard list of 2,048.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.628
Pick 12 of them, and the number

00:03:59.628 --> 00:04:02.565
of possible wallets is a 39-digit number.

00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:04.895
That's why nobody can guess your seed phrase.

00:04:06.195 --> 00:04:07.508
Which leads to the most

00:04:07.508 --> 00:04:09.450
important idea in this module.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.465
Your seed phrase can't be guessed.

00:04:11.465 --> 00:04:13.316
So the only way to lose it is

00:04:13.316 --> 00:04:15.413
to reveal it: by typing it into a

00:04:15.413 --> 00:04:18.745
fake website, photographing it, storing it online, or

00:04:18.745 --> 00:04:21.019
handing it to someone who asked.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.224
Never share it.

00:04:22.224 --> 00:04:24.149
Nobody legitimate will ever ask.

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:27.294
Word four: exchange.

00:04:27.294 --> 00:04:29.954
A company that swaps your money for crypto.

00:04:29.954 --> 00:04:31.407
It's your front door, and it's

00:04:31.407 --> 00:04:33.114
where you'll buy and cash out.

00:04:33.114 --> 00:04:35.017
But while your crypto sits on an

00:04:35.017 --> 00:04:37.509
exchange, the exchange holds the keys.

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:39.801
In your own wallet, you hold the keys.

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:42.846
That difference is the whole of Lesson 0.4.

00:04:44.146 --> 00:04:45.856
Word five: gas.

00:04:45.856 --> 00:04:47.931
The network fee for a transaction.

00:04:47.931 --> 00:04:49.926
Think of it as a postage stamp.

00:04:49.926 --> 00:04:52.465
Every transaction pays a small fee, in the

00:04:52.465 --> 00:04:55.300
network's own coin, to the computers that check

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.076
it and add it to a block.

00:04:57.076 --> 00:04:58.961
And the stamp is usually spent

00:04:58.961 --> 00:05:00.786
even if the transaction fails.

00:05:02.086 --> 00:05:03.589
Gas isn't a fixed price,

00:05:03.589 --> 00:05:05.391
and this chart shows why.

00:05:05.391 --> 00:05:07.926
It's an auction for space in the next block.

00:05:07.926 --> 00:05:10.571
When the network is quiet, the stamp is cheap.

00:05:10.571 --> 00:05:12.203
When everyone rushes to send

00:05:12.203 --> 00:05:13.966
at once, the price climbs.

00:05:13.966 --> 00:05:16.363
These numbers are illustrative, but the

00:05:16.363 --> 00:05:18.701
shape is real: fees follow demand.

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:20.758
So if a fee looks high, you can

00:05:20.758 --> 00:05:23.650
often simply wait, or use a cheaper network,

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.156
which you'll learn about in Lesson 0.6.

00:05:27.456 --> 00:05:29.291
Quick check on the five words.

00:05:29.291 --> 00:05:31.256
What does a wallet actually hold:

00:05:31.256 --> 00:05:32.701
your coins, or your keys?

00:05:37.121 --> 00:05:38.836
The answer: your keys.

00:05:38.836 --> 00:05:40.861
The coins live on the blockchain.

00:05:40.861 --> 00:05:43.436
The wallet holds the keys that prove they're yours.

00:05:44.735 --> 00:05:46.740
Now, what you need before you start.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.905
First, four simple tools.

00:05:49.905 --> 00:05:53.015
A phone, for your authenticator app and your wallet.

00:05:53.015 --> 00:05:56.216
A computer, for the bigger screens and careful checks.

00:05:56.216 --> 00:06:00.380
Photo ID, because regulated exchanges must check who you

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.041
are, a process called KYC, know your customer.

00:06:04.041 --> 00:06:05.703
And a bank account, to move

00:06:05.703 --> 00:06:07.246
ordinary money in and out.

00:06:08.547 --> 00:06:09.732
Second, money.

00:06:09.732 --> 00:06:11.471
$50 to $200 that you can

00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:13.927
afford to lose while you learn.

00:06:13.927 --> 00:06:17.492
Here's one example of how $100 might be split.

00:06:17.492 --> 00:06:20.279
About $40 of ETH, the coin that pays

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:23.442
gas, and that you'll use for practice.

00:06:23.442 --> 00:06:26.653
About $45 of USDC, a stablecoin, a

00:06:26.653 --> 00:06:30.807
digital dollar designed to hold its value.

00:06:30.807 --> 00:06:32.814
And about $15 set aside for

00:06:32.814 --> 00:06:35.480
spreads, fees and test transfers.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.195
That last slice matters.

00:06:37.195 --> 00:06:38.736
Test transfers are part of

00:06:38.736 --> 00:06:40.105
the lesson, not a waste.

00:06:41.405 --> 00:06:42.820
Why so small?

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.172
Because on a blockchain, a mistake costs

00:06:45.172 --> 00:06:47.710
whatever you sent, and there's no undo.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:49.048
So look at what the same

00:06:49.048 --> 00:06:51.115
mistake costs at different sizes.

00:06:51.115 --> 00:06:54.995
A wrong-network send of a $10 test costs $10.

00:06:54.995 --> 00:06:58.290
With a $100 learning budget, it costs $100.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.555
With $5,000, it costs $5,000.

00:07:01.555 --> 00:07:03.500
The mistake is identical.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.315
Only the size changes.

00:07:05.315 --> 00:07:07.252
Small amounts make your mistakes cheap

00:07:07.252 --> 00:07:09.040
while your habits are still forming.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.700
And third, time.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.205
2 to 3 hours in total,

00:07:13.205 --> 00:07:15.010
spread over a few days.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.775
Here's what that can look like.

00:07:16.775 --> 00:07:20.600
Day 1, about 30 minutes: your passwords and security.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.855
Day 2, about 35: the exchange account.

00:07:23.855 --> 00:07:25.132
Day 3, about 40: a

00:07:25.132 --> 00:07:27.180
first buy and your wallet.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.600
Day 4, about 30: the

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.065
restore test and first transfer.

00:07:31.065 --> 00:07:33.342
And Day 5, about 25: practice

00:07:33.342 --> 00:07:36.070
DeFi and your security baseline.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.836
Short sessions beat one long one, because you'll

00:07:38.836 --> 00:07:41.489
be calmer, and calm people make fewer mistakes.

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:44.064
Your first safe step.

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:48.109
Your first step doesn't involve crypto at all.

00:07:48.109 --> 00:07:50.999
Read Lesson 0.1, then secure your email

00:07:50.999 --> 00:07:55.344
with a unique password and authenticator-app two-factor.

00:07:55.344 --> 00:07:56.939
No money needed yet.

00:07:56.939 --> 00:07:58.394
Here's why it comes first.

00:07:59.694 --> 00:08:02.719
Your email is the master key to everything else.

00:08:02.719 --> 00:08:04.854
Watch how an attack actually works.

00:08:04.854 --> 00:08:07.114
Someone gets into your email, often with

00:08:07.114 --> 00:08:09.564
a password leaked from another website.

00:08:09.564 --> 00:08:12.029
They click forgot password on your exchange.

00:08:12.029 --> 00:08:14.063
The reset link arrives in your email,

00:08:14.063 --> 00:08:15.969
so they choose a new password.

00:08:15.969 --> 00:08:17.262
And now they control your

00:08:17.262 --> 00:08:19.204
exchange account, and your money.

00:08:19.204 --> 00:08:20.837
Lock the email, and this whole

00:08:20.837 --> 00:08:22.469
chain breaks at the first step.

00:08:23.769 --> 00:08:25.354
So, two changes.

00:08:25.354 --> 00:08:28.106
First, a unique password, created and stored

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:30.124
by a password manager, so a leak

00:08:30.124 --> 00:08:32.809
somewhere else can't unlock your email.

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:37.114
Second, two-factor authentication using an authenticator app.

00:08:37.114 --> 00:08:39.064
Why not text messages?

00:08:39.064 --> 00:08:41.602
Because a criminal can talk a phone company

00:08:41.602 --> 00:08:44.324
into moving your number to their SIM card.

00:08:44.324 --> 00:08:46.198
That's called a SIM swap, and it

00:08:46.198 --> 00:08:48.599
means your codes go straight to them.

00:08:48.599 --> 00:08:51.064
An authenticator app stays on your phone.

00:08:51.064 --> 00:08:53.859
And save your backup codes somewhere safe, offline.

00:08:55.159 --> 00:08:57.704
Here's the first safe step as a checklist.

00:08:57.704 --> 00:08:59.594
Install a password manager.

00:08:59.594 --> 00:09:02.179
Give your email a long, unique password.

00:09:02.179 --> 00:09:04.574
Turn on authenticator-app two-factor.

00:09:04.574 --> 00:09:06.679
Save the backup codes offline.

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:07.762
Then do the same for

00:09:07.762 --> 00:09:09.464
every account that touches money.

00:09:10.764 --> 00:09:11.854
Quick check.

00:09:11.854 --> 00:09:15.549
Why is text-message two-factor weaker than an authenticator app?

00:09:19.969 --> 00:09:22.202
The answer: a criminal can take over

00:09:22.202 --> 00:09:24.314
your phone number with a SIM swap,

00:09:24.314 --> 00:09:26.304
and then receive your codes.

00:09:26.304 --> 00:09:29.024
Authenticator codes are made on your own phone.

00:09:30.324 --> 00:09:31.419
The mastery ladder.

00:09:32.719 --> 00:09:35.894
Here's how you'll measure your progress through Module 0.

00:09:35.894 --> 00:09:37.009
Three rungs.

00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:39.687
Beginner: you can explain blockchain, wallet

00:09:39.687 --> 00:09:41.832
and seed phrase in plain words.

00:09:41.832 --> 00:09:44.711
Practitioner: you have a secured exchange account

00:09:44.711 --> 00:09:47.372
and a wallet whose restore you've tested.

00:09:47.372 --> 00:09:50.461
And Master: you've completed the whole Day-1 Setup Kit,

00:09:50.461 --> 00:09:52.667
and you could coach a friend through it.

00:09:53.966 --> 00:09:56.266
Notice what the top rung asks for.

00:09:56.266 --> 00:09:57.781
Not a big balance.

00:09:57.781 --> 00:09:59.246
Not a clever trade.

00:09:59.246 --> 00:10:00.861
Just a finished checklist, and

00:10:00.861 --> 00:10:02.411
the ability to teach it.

00:10:02.411 --> 00:10:04.494
Teaching someone else is the best test

00:10:04.494 --> 00:10:06.791
of whether you really understand something.

00:10:08.091 --> 00:10:10.691
Here's the road ahead, lesson by lesson.

00:10:10.691 --> 00:10:14.923
0.1, money, ledgers and why blockchains exist.

00:10:14.923 --> 00:10:18.413
0.2, opening and securing an exchange.

00:10:18.413 --> 00:10:21.853
0.3, your first buy without overpaying.

00:10:21.853 --> 00:10:24.428
0.4, who holds the keys.

00:10:24.428 --> 00:10:27.883
0.5, setting up and backing up your wallet.

00:10:27.883 --> 00:10:31.718
0.6, networks, gas and your first transfer.

00:10:31.718 --> 00:10:34.758
0.7, your first DeFi steps.

00:10:34.758 --> 00:10:37.110
And 0.8, your security baseline

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:38.948
and the language of DeFi.

00:10:40.248 --> 00:10:43.333
And here's the whole module, recapped as one loop.

00:10:43.333 --> 00:10:44.948
You secure your accounts.

00:10:44.948 --> 00:10:47.373
You open and lock down an exchange.

00:10:47.373 --> 00:10:49.148
You make a small first buy.

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:50.613
You move it into a wallet

00:10:50.613 --> 00:10:52.828
you control, with a tested backup.

00:10:52.828 --> 00:10:54.888
You send one careful transfer.

00:10:54.888 --> 00:10:58.033
And you take one small, practiced step into DeFi.

00:10:58.033 --> 00:10:59.868
Do those six things, in order,

00:10:59.868 --> 00:11:01.288
and Module Zero is done.

00:11:02.588 --> 00:11:04.953
So when have you mastered this module?

00:11:04.953 --> 00:11:06.207
When every box of the

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:07.988
Day-1 Setup Kit is ticked.

00:11:07.988 --> 00:11:10.906
It's a printable checklist in the Start here chapter,

00:11:10.906 --> 00:11:13.338
and it follows the same order as the lessons.

00:11:14.638 --> 00:11:16.563
Here's the route it takes you on.

00:11:16.563 --> 00:11:17.573
Accounts.

00:11:17.573 --> 00:11:18.668
Exchange.

00:11:18.668 --> 00:11:19.823
First buy.

00:11:19.823 --> 00:11:20.763
Wallet.

00:11:20.763 --> 00:11:22.098
First transfer.

00:11:22.098 --> 00:11:23.483
Practice DeFi.

00:11:23.483 --> 00:11:25.288
And your security baseline.

00:11:25.288 --> 00:11:28.043
Seven steps, a small amount, over a few days.

00:11:29.343 --> 00:11:30.528
Last check. What's

00:11:30.528 --> 00:11:33.153
the sign that you've mastered Module 0? [[pause 4]]

00:11:37.573 --> 00:11:39.569
The answer: every box of the Day-1

00:11:39.569 --> 00:11:41.565
Setup Kit is ticked, and you could

00:11:41.565 --> 00:11:43.048
coach a friend through it.

00:11:44.348 --> 00:11:46.553
Three questions every beginner asks.

00:11:47.853 --> 00:11:49.063
Question one.

00:11:49.063 --> 00:11:50.838
What if I lose my phone?

00:11:50.838 --> 00:11:53.733
This is exactly why the seed phrase exists.

00:11:53.733 --> 00:11:55.778
You buy a new phone or device.

00:11:55.778 --> 00:11:57.733
You install the same wallet app.

00:11:57.733 --> 00:12:00.358
You choose restore, instead of create new.

00:12:00.358 --> 00:12:02.558
You type in your seed phrase, in order.

00:12:02.558 --> 00:12:05.006
And every key, every address, every balance

00:12:05.006 --> 00:12:07.397
comes back, because they were never really

00:12:07.397 --> 00:12:09.233
"in" the old phone to begin with.

00:12:10.533 --> 00:12:11.778
Question two.

00:12:11.778 --> 00:12:14.628
Is my money insured, like a bank account?

00:12:14.628 --> 00:12:16.303
Here's the honest answer.

00:12:16.303 --> 00:12:19.492
A bank deposit is typically protected by a government

00:12:19.492 --> 00:12:22.214
scheme, up to a limit, if the bank fails.

00:12:22.214 --> 00:12:25.124
Crypto in your own wallet has no such scheme.

00:12:25.124 --> 00:12:26.396
There's no one to call,

00:12:26.396 --> 00:12:27.809
and no claim to file.

00:12:27.809 --> 00:12:30.252
That's exactly why this module spends so

00:12:30.252 --> 00:12:32.516
much time on backups and safe habits:

00:12:32.516 --> 00:12:34.839
they're the only insurance that exists.

00:12:36.139 --> 00:12:37.409
Question three.

00:12:37.409 --> 00:12:40.054
What if the price crashes right after I buy?

00:12:40.054 --> 00:12:42.809
Here's an honest example, not a prediction.

00:12:42.809 --> 00:12:45.605
Crypto prices move far more than most

00:12:45.605 --> 00:12:48.032
people expect: down 30% in a bad

00:12:48.032 --> 00:12:50.981
month is not unusual for some coins.

00:12:50.981 --> 00:12:53.277
That's exactly why your first buy is

00:12:53.277 --> 00:12:56.070
a small, learning-sized amount, and why this

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.790
module teaches habits, not price calls.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:00.148
The goal of Module 0

00:13:00.148 --> 00:13:02.065
isn't to time the market.

00:13:02.065 --> 00:13:03.345
It's to get you set up

00:13:03.345 --> 00:13:05.405
safely, whatever the price does next.

00:13:06.705 --> 00:13:08.293
Before we wrap the ladder up,

00:13:08.293 --> 00:13:10.445
one more picture worth remembering.

00:13:10.445 --> 00:13:13.416
Almost every beginner loss traces back to one

00:13:13.416 --> 00:13:15.806
of four doors: a lost seed phrase, a

00:13:15.806 --> 00:13:18.584
transfer sent on the wrong network, a fake

00:13:18.584 --> 00:13:21.855
site or fake support agent, or simple rushing.

00:13:21.855 --> 00:13:24.387
Module Zero is built to close all four

00:13:24.387 --> 00:13:26.855
before you ever move a serious amount.

00:13:28.155 --> 00:13:29.957
One more honest answer, because

00:13:29.957 --> 00:13:31.270
it comes up a lot.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.945
Can this program guarantee I'll make money?

00:13:33.945 --> 00:13:36.496
No. Nobody can, and anyone who tells you

00:13:36.496 --> 00:13:39.472
otherwise is a warning sign, not a promise.

00:13:39.472 --> 00:13:42.242
What this program guarantees is a process: a

00:13:42.242 --> 00:13:45.566
safe setup, clear checklists, and habits that protect

00:13:45.566 --> 00:13:47.967
you whether the market goes up or down.

00:13:49.267 --> 00:13:50.717
One final check.

00:13:50.717 --> 00:13:53.535
Someone offers to "guarantee" you 20% a

00:13:53.535 --> 00:13:56.089
month if you send them crypto to

00:13:56.089 --> 00:13:57.004
manage. What should

00:14:01.424 --> 00:14:03.149
you do? [[pause 4]]

00:14:03.149 --> 00:14:05.263
The answer: walk away. No result is

00:14:05.263 --> 00:14:08.374
ever guaranteed, and nobody legitimate takes custody

00:14:08.374 --> 00:14:10.312
of your crypto through a message.

00:14:11.612 --> 00:14:14.377
Before you move on, do this lesson's checklist.

00:14:14.377 --> 00:14:15.966
A phone, a computer, ID

00:14:15.966 --> 00:14:17.987
and a bank account, ready.

00:14:17.987 --> 00:14:20.017
A learning budget of $50 to

00:14:20.017 --> 00:14:22.347
$200 you can afford to lose.

00:14:22.347 --> 00:14:23.684
2 to 3 hours blocked

00:14:23.684 --> 00:14:25.257
out over a few days.

00:14:25.257 --> 00:14:27.086
And your email secured, with a

00:14:27.086 --> 00:14:29.977
unique password and authenticator-app two-factor.

00:14:31.277 --> 00:14:34.322
This is education, not financial advice.

00:14:34.322 --> 00:14:36.602
Crypto is volatile, you can lose some

00:14:36.602 --> 00:14:38.222
or all of what you put in,

00:14:38.222 --> 00:14:40.322
and no results are guaranteed.

00:14:40.322 --> 00:14:43.226
And nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:14:43.226 --> 00:14:46.253
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:14:47.553 --> 00:14:49.248
That's the Mastery Starter.

00:14:49.248 --> 00:14:51.860
You now have the whole module in your head:

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.530
the journey of $100, the five words, what to

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:57.439
have ready, your first safe step, and the ladder

00:14:57.439 --> 00:14:59.638
that tells you when you're done.

00:14:59.638 --> 00:15:01.957
Next up, Lesson 0.1: Money,

00:15:01.957 --> 00:15:05.074
ledgers and why blockchains exist.

00:15:05.074 --> 00:15:05.859
See you there.
